10. Open points
---------------

  O1  HOW THE TYPING RULES ACCOUNT FOR THE REFLECTION CAST (§4.2). Since a shared
      subterm may read as A through one occurrence and B through another, [t] is
      no longer a function of the term, and `term_utils._term_type` needs an
      OCCURRENCE argument. §2's three rules cover unreflected material only. The
      restatement is mechanical but has not been made.

  O2  WHETHER THE REFLECTION CAST IS CONSTRAINED (§4.2, §7.4). Nothing relates A
      and B to the type C the user was standing at, so reflection may retype a
      shared subterm to anything. Candidate constraint: require arrows out of C,
      i.e. EXISTS_RELATION(C,A) and EXISTS_RELATION(C,B). Bears directly on the
      knowledge-graph-realisability claim of §7.4.

  O3  THE FAN-IN LEVEL'S MEANING for a reading, bearing on §7.3(a): a further
      reflection creates a NEW fan-in at its own level rather than adding a third
      parent, so fan-out > 2 is not reachable. Whether that distinction is
      semantically meaningful for a reading — as opposed to for the reducer, where
      levels keep independent sharings from interfering — is open.

  O4  WHETHER A MERGE / CO-REFERENCE STEP IS WANTED, to reach §7.3(c). It would
      consume two pointers and yield one — "these two things I have attended to
      separately are the same thing". It is NOT needed for argument-position
      sharing, which §7.2 constructs from the existing steps.

  O5  WHETHER THE SELECTED G(t2) MUST BE JOINED BY AN ARROW THAT EXISTS in the
      knowledge graph when it is a closed reading rather than an entity. A
      closed reading is not an entity, so the existence check that applies to
      entity operands has no evident counterpart; only the type precondition is
      checked here.

  O6  COPY VS. SHARE when a closed reading is grafted. Copying keeps closed
      readings immutable; sharing makes later changes visible in every reading
      that grafted them. Copy is the safer default.

  O7  WHETHER A CLOSED READING CARRIES ITS ONTOLOGY SET (§8.3). Initialisation
      form (b) takes "the ontologies of R1", but a closed reading is the graph
      alone (§1). Either closing persists them, or re-opening re-derives them
      from the root pointer by enrichment.

  O8  PARTLY RESOLVED 2026-08-18 (§8.6). Option 1 now handles a CLOSED READING
      operand: it pairs each current ontology with each of R1's, substituting R1's
      ontology-term for the variable at that position and requiring rule 1 to fire.
      Still open: (a) whether OPTION 2 admits a closed reading at all — the
      definition gives no case for it, so option 2 currently means entity-only,
      which if intended belongs in §4.1 as a restriction on the step; (b) the
      ABSTRACTION TITLE operand, which has no case in either option and may need
      none, being plausibly covered by enrichment substituting the title's own
      positive form (but not branching over all abstractions of that type, since
      the user named one); (c) review notes RN2, RN3 and RN5 in §8.6.

  O9  HOW THE ONTOLOGY LAYER MIRRORS THE NEW REFLECTION (§8.7). The reflection
      update rule still tests for a subtree app(tb,tc) with the branch types on
      separate operands, which the reading no longer builds: reflection now emits
      app(t,t) with the types CAST onto the two occurrences. The rule needs
      restating against the new form.

  O10 THE ONTOLOGY SET NEEDS A SIZE BOUND — now a PREREQUISITE, not an
      optimisation. Enrichment terminates (§8.4) but branches over every
      abstraction of matching type at every pointer on every step; option 1's
      closed-reading case multiplies by |R1.ontologies| (§8.6 review note RN4); and
      since the 2026-08-18 redefinition, contraction refutes almost nothing
      (§8.8). Nothing balances the growth. A cap plus a deterministic eviction
      policy is required; O11 proposes what to rank by.

  O11 WHETHER NON-FIRING SURVIVAL NEEDS A CONFIRMATION COUNT. §8.6 now keeps an
      ontology in which rule 1 does not fire, which is right in distinguishing
      CONTRADICTED from SILENT but leaves contraction refuting almost nothing
      (§8.8). Candidate: count firings per ontology and let O10's cap evict by that
      count, so survival tracks confirmation and "better approximation" stays
      measurable. The alternative is to rule that silence is fatal. See review note
      R1 in §8.6.
